---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { APK } from '../data/apk';
---
<Base title="Privacy & Permissions" description="What MJ PDF 3.1.0 asks for on Android and what to know about privacy, based on the supplied APK." path="/privacy/" crumbs={[{name:'Privacy',path:'/privacy/'}]}>
  <div class="wrap page-h"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> / Privacy</nav>
    <h1 style="font-size:clamp(2rem,5vw,3rem);margin-top:1rem">Privacy &amp; permissions</h1>
    <p class="lead">What we can verify, stated plainly.</p></div>
  <section class="sec" style="padding-top:20px"><div class="wrap" style="max-width:860px">
    <h2>What is verified</h2>
    <ul class="checks"><li>MJ PDF is open source (GPL-3.0), so its code can be inspected.</li><li>No advertising is part of the app.</li><li>PDFs are read locally on your device.</li></ul>
    <div class="note"><strong>About trackers.</strong> The project's own documentation states that Exodus Privacy reports a tracker in MJ PDF. The supplied APK also bundles the ACRA crash-reporting library. This site therefore does not claim "zero tracking" or "no data collection". Review the source code or the Exodus report for details.</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:40px">Permissions declared in the 3.1.0 APK</h2>
    <table class="spec"><tbody>{APK.permissions.map(([p,d]) => <tr><th scope="row" class="mono">{p}</th><td>{d}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table>
    <h2 style="margin-top:40px">This website</h2>
    <p>This website is a static site. It has no accounts, forms or advertising scripts.</p>
  </div></section>
</Base>
